to really get a definitive sense of the path

00:00:40.649 --> 00:00:42.059
forward for this nomination.

00:00:42.059 --> 00:00:45.120
Could Bernie get 50% plus one?

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.219
Is it more likely that there will not be any
candidate getting to that threshold or far

00:00:49.219 --> 00:00:54.809
less likely, but still possible will some
other candidate get to 50% plus one before

00:00:54.809 --> 00:00:55.809
the convention.

00:00:55.809 --> 00:01:00.100
Now, I'm not going to do a full review of
the debate last night with a whole bunch of

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:05.720
clips because quite frankly, it was very similar
to last week's debate except with worse moderation.

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:11.030
It was again, this kind of ultimate fighting
type debate with many candidates coming in

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:15.799
and we knew they would do it going after Bernie
Sanders, who's now the presumptive front runner.

00:01:15.799 --> 00:01:18.750
Uh, the moderation was absolutely atrocious.

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:20.970
People constantly talking over each other.

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:25.729
That applies both to multiple moderators talking
at the same time while multiple candidates

00:01:25.729 --> 00:01:30.320
talk at the same time, uh, cutting people
short of their 75 seconds.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:34.570
But at the same time letting some candidates
go too long.

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:36.100
It was a complete and total mess.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:39.859
But the overall assessment of what went on
last night for me is that Joe Biden came in

00:01:39.859 --> 00:01:41.260
with a bunch of energy.

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:46.700
He didn't say anything different, but he at
least looked more energetic than he has before.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:48.670
Maybe he would get himself a small boost.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:55.130
The crowd was not particularly friendly to
Bernie and there's a backstory there as well.

00:01:55.130 --> 00:02:01.509
Um, a lot more booing of Bernie and cheering
of Joe Biden and Michael Bloomberg, and that's

00:02:01.509 --> 00:02:06.680
not reflective of anything other than the
people that got seats in that room.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:10.069
Some of them paying quite a bit in order to
be in that room.

00:02:10.069 --> 00:02:14.890
I don't think anybody really did particular
damage to their own campaign.

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:17.540
I don't expect polling to move very much.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:22.980
I think if anything, Biden may be gets a slight
boost because he just seemed more alive last

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:23.980
night.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:28.100
But it's important to know, and we're going
to get to the polling data later.

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:34.120
As bad as Michael Bloomberg's performance
was in last week's debate, Bloomberg was totally

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.410
